 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The bottom-up build algorithm is $O(N)$ with respect to the total number of voxels [S9]. It is approximately two times faster than top-down construction because it visits each data value only once [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Space overhead is generally acceptable if large macro voxels are created. Maximum overhead is approximately $v \times b$, where $v$ is the total number of voxels and $b$ is the number of bytes used to store information about each internal tree node [S1, unofficial]. However, building an expanded full octree structure can occupy a footprint of four times the raw volume size, requiring significant memory (e.g., nearly 40 GB for Richtmyer-Meshkov data) [S10, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a single core of a 16-core 2.4 GHz Opteron workstation, building a single timestep of the $30^3$ heptane volume requires 8 seconds with negligible memory footprint [S10, unofficial]. Building a timestep of Richtmyer-Meshkov data requires 45 minutes [S10, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High compression ratio for coherent data; fast construction time; supports both lossless and lossy modes; integrates well with ray tracing via min/max bounds [S1, unofficial][S7, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression can degrade image quality for 8-bit data [S1, unofficial]; parallel implementation requires careful synchronization [S3, unofficial]; memory footprint can be high for uncompressed or partially compressed data [S10, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for large, coherent volume datasets where compression and fast traversal are priorities, and where the data can tolerate some lossiness or is naturally quantized [S1, unofficial][S9].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for data requiring high-fidelity visualization with small error tolerances (e.g., 8-bit medical data) [S1, unofficial], or when memory is extremely constrained and the volume is highly heterogeneous [S10, unofficial].
